00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:54.000
But I want to go back to an interview I did a&nbsp;
few years ago with Gretchen Boki about exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:02.320
the relationship between the electrical grid, a&nbsp;
green alternative, renewable energy, the conflicts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:08.320
that exist because of business interests&nbsp;
and politics and so many other things. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:16.000
could not be a more relevant time to revisit this&nbsp;
interview with Gretchen. Let's get right into it.&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.000
I'm joined today by Gretchen Boki, who is author&nbsp;
of The Grid The Fraying Wires Between Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.520
and Our Energy Future. Gretchen, it's so great&nbsp;
to talk to you. We talk so much about the move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:33.120
towards alternative and renewable sources of&nbsp;
energy. We talk about the political implications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.080
and the sort of economic implications. But one&nbsp;
thing that's often missing from that conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.560
is sort of the reality of the electrical&nbsp;
grid that we're working with and some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.280
the limitations that exist. And we'll sort&nbsp;
of have to be dealt with if we really want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:53.200
to move away from fossil fuels, which I believe&nbsp;
that we should be trying to do. Let's start as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.920
broadly as you want to. I mean, what are some&nbsp;
of the limitations that need to be considered?&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.720
Well, I think the main thing that people don't&nbsp;
quite grasp when there's when when when thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:10.320
about the energy transition is that electricity&nbsp;
can't really be stored right now. And what that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:17.120
means is that as it's made, it's used, it's this&nbsp;
very instantaneous product. So as it's made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.960
it's used. And that means that if we make it&nbsp;
with something like coal, it's a very steady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.720
production. And if we make it with something&nbsp;
like wind, it's a very unsteady production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:33.600
because the amount of electricity changes as&nbsp;
the as the rate of the wind blowing changes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.520
And so that's called variable generation. And&nbsp;
it's the more of it that we put into our grid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.320
we tend to think of it as renewables. But not&nbsp;
all renewables are variable. And the more we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:51.120
put of it, we put into the grid, the the harder&nbsp;
it is to manage this sort of perfect balancing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.800
of how much electricity we use in any instant&nbsp;
and how much electricity we produce in that same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:03.200
instant because electricity is essentially&nbsp;
instantaneous. So almost never more than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.920
minute between when it's made and when it's used.
And this is particularly relevant for people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:14.400
already have or are thinking of moving to solar&nbsp;
power in their home, unless you actually have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.640
a means of storing electricity.&nbsp;
Right. Unless you have an actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:25.440
battery set up, any excess electricity&nbsp;
that is harvested by your home solar system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.840
is not stored for your future use, but rather goes&nbsp;
back into the grid and as you mentioned, is almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.560
instantaneously used by someone else. I mean,&nbsp;
is that generally the way that it works?&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:40.080
Yeah, and I think that's one of the things that&nbsp;
I was really confused about for a long time was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.040
why people could have diesel generators and&nbsp;
seem to be like when there was a blackout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.680
they would be able to power things in their&nbsp;
house. But if you have solar panels, you can't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.760
And like many people, I thought that was&nbsp;
something that the utilities were somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.720
scheming to make it that you so you couldn't go&nbsp;
off the grid completely. But actually, diesel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.760
generators have all these very heavy pieces inside&nbsp;
of them. And so when you turn on a light switch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.120
there's a kind of inertial force inside the&nbsp;
generator that allows it to balance your house.&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.760
So the the power you have on or turning&nbsp;
on or turning off at any moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.240
it has to be balanced by the amount of electricity&nbsp;
that generator is producing. So, again, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:24.720
fossil fuel and then this heavy piece of equipment&nbsp;
enable you to run your house even without storage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:31.440
whereas solar panels, obviously, you're not going&nbsp;
to have any any power at night. But even so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.280
you turn on your light. There's no way for for a&nbsp;
solar panel to adjust to that without some kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:41.600
storage. So you turn on a light, you're asking for&nbsp;
more electricity from that system. But that system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:48.320
is just producing electricity based on how much&nbsp;
sun is hitting it at that moment. And we don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:54.160
use we don't even think in terms of like, OK, it's&nbsp;
midday and it's really sunny. So I'm going to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.160
everything right now. Right. Our fridge turns on,&nbsp;
our freezer turns on, it goes off. We turn on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:04.960
lights, we turn them off. And all of that has to&nbsp;
be balanced in some way by something. And right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:10.560
now that something is the grid and people like the&nbsp;
grid, it's not there's not a there's not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.880
a strong movement, I think, against the grid. But&nbsp;
there is this sense of like, why can't I be energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:22.400
independent? And the reason for that is when you&nbsp;
turn on the light and you have solar panels, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.520
extra electricity needs to come from somewhere.&nbsp;
So it comes through the grid. And if the grid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:32.800
is down, then it doesn't it doesn't come from&nbsp;
anywhere and is part of the path forward on this.&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:40.320
I mean, Elon Musk from Tesla has talked about&nbsp;
the home battery systems that they're working on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.640
That within a few years, he says, will&nbsp;
be relatively affordable. I mean, is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:49.120
is that the type of solution that you&nbsp;
see is the most likely to this problem?&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:57.040
I think it's very hard to see the future because&nbsp;
I think that people really like the idea of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:02.640
part of a larger electricity system. It's it was&nbsp;
very rare in my research that I found people that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:08.240
wanted to wanted some kind of energy, want it to&nbsp;
be isolated all the time. They're happy to produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.280
for the grid solar panels in particular, like&nbsp;
very happy to produce for the grid during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:19.600
daytime to to draw from the grid at night time.&nbsp;
So I think that that there is a possibility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.080
certainly with those home battery storage systems,&nbsp;
that you could be energy independent all the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.720
So you wouldn't need the grid at all. But I think&nbsp;
what's more likely is that people will start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:35.200
use those so that they have power when the power&nbsp;
is out on the larger system. And and that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:40.320
a lot of what was happening when I was doing my&nbsp;
research, is that a lot of the frustration I found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.360
with the electricity system, part of it was&nbsp;
this feeling of like we want more renewables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:50.000
which is which is very hard to manage. And I think&nbsp;
there's a lot of work going in that direction by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.160
people who do manage the grid right now, but not&nbsp;
fast enough. There's there's a lot of anger with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:02.160
the utilities in particular for not making that.
And quickly enough, but the other big frustration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:08.400
was, my power is out all the time, I have solar&nbsp;
panels, why can't I just have my power beyond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:12.800
like, why do I have to also have&nbsp;
a diesel backup generator? So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:20.560
this movement from producing power independently,&nbsp;
but also trying to be green at home and wanting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:26.240
the larger electricity system to be green as well.
So if we step aside a little bit from the storage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.400
aspect of it, I'd love to talk a little bit&nbsp;
about some of the other ways in which the grid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:37.040
is relatively susceptible to outside forces.&nbsp;
We have mostly in this country, elevated power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:43.040
lines where anything from weather to a squirrel&nbsp;
can cause pretty significant interruptions. Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.320
that one of the biggest sort of liabilities&nbsp;
in the American electrical grid right now?&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.960
You mean the the the power&nbsp;
lines being above ground?&nbsp;

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:58.320
Yeah, it's certainly certainly it's it is in part&nbsp;
because the greatest threat to the grid right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.200
is growing trees. So if they were underground,&nbsp;
that would be less of a problem. It's really hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.133
to cut back all of the foliage that is just&nbsp;
up and coming over this huge nation. Right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.133 --> 00:08:13.920
Is always just going up and up and up and sort of&nbsp;
grabbing at power lines. So, yes, it's not always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:19.440
the answer to bury, though. If you have someplace&nbsp;
like Florida with a high sort of a high tidal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:24.400
I don't know. I think the word is scale,&nbsp;
but the water comes at very high tidally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:29.600
or when when it's very wet, you don't want to&nbsp;
bury you don't want to bury lines and you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:34.000
even want critical infrastructure at ground level.&nbsp;
You want to start putting stuff up. You want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:39.760
put your substations on hills. So for some places,&nbsp;
it's quite it's quite a good idea to bury lines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:46.400
In other places, it's a good idea. The grid is&nbsp;
weirdly local. So we have three of them in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:50.560
US and they're each gigantic through these huge&nbsp;
machines or sort of one for the west, one for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:56.480
the east and one for Texas. But nevertheless,&nbsp;
it's also extremely local in how it's designed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:01.120
how different states have been keeping it up, the&nbsp;
kinds of laws there are laws that are federal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:06.800
but there are many laws that are very particular&nbsp;
to states or even to counties or municipalities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:13.040
So taking into account the weather in particular&nbsp;
places matters for answering that question. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.440
it's also extremely expensive to bury lines.
And that also goes towards what sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:22.080
underlying this, which is I don't know if I would&nbsp;
call it a jurisdictional issue, but certainly one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:28.560
of ownership, which is that there is not like a&nbsp;
single entity that owns or controls or maintains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.240
the grid. Right. I mean, as you're sort&nbsp;
of talking about, you're talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:36.160
municipalities and a lot of smaller&nbsp;
entities that are part of this grid.&nbsp;

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:41.440
Mm hmm. And even manage their own grid.&nbsp;
There are many municipal like Los Angeles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:47.440
for example, has it manages its own grid, even&nbsp;
though that grid is connected into the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.560
gigantic grid, which covers the whole of the West.&nbsp;
It's still that particular piece of it, like where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:57.440
they're buying electricity from, how much they're&nbsp;
paying for it, who's maintaining the lines. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:02.800
of that is is managed by the municipality. And&nbsp;
some municipal goods are very, very small. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:08.080
talk about in the book Rancho Cucamonga, which has&nbsp;
one streetlight, but they have a municipal power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:14.800
municipal power, governance, and people, again,&nbsp;
are sort of organizing suburbs, et cetera, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:23.040
they get frustrated with the rate of change to a&nbsp;
more renewable, renewably powered system. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:27.360
they'll just get towns or suburbs together and&nbsp;
they'll they'll aggregate, it's called from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:33.360
big grid and take over their own little piece of&nbsp;
it to manage it for themselves. So that's sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.560
that's how that happens legally in the state.&nbsp;
So it's both big and very, very small, very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:45.120
very local and absolutely nationally gigantic.
And I think that's where you see a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.960
excitement about what's happening right&nbsp;
now. I know most people don't really see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:54.400
the grid or think about the grid, but it's it's&nbsp;
changing radically at this exact moment in time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.960
And lots of people are causing that&nbsp;
change to happen in different ways.&nbsp;

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:03.280
Well, it is a hugely important issue,&nbsp;
particularly when we have the conversation about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.960
when and how will we get off of fossil&nbsp;
fuels. It's something that that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:10.720
not be more important. Again, we've been&nbsp;
talking about the book The Grid, the Fraying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:15.120
Wires Between Americans and Our Energy Future.
And we've been speaking with the book's author,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:20.160
Gretchen Boki. Thanks so much for talking to&nbsp;
us about that. Yeah, my pleasure. Thank you.

